"""Non-calculation conversations: concept questions, advice requests, off-topic, greetings.

These teach the model *when not to call a tool*. Answers avoid specific figures
that change over time (contribution limits, tax brackets) and contain no
computed numbers, so they pass the agent's grounding check.
"""
from ..responses import CAPABILITIES

# (train questions, test questions, answer)
CONCEPTS = [
    (["What's the difference between APR and APY?", "APR vs APY?", "Is APY the same thing as APR?"],
     ["Can you explain how APR and APY differ?"],
     "APR (annual percentage rate) is the yearly interest rate before compounding. APY (annual percentage yield) includes the effect of compounding, so it shows what you actually earn or pay over a year. When interest compounds more than once a year, the APY is higher than the APR. If you give me a rate and how often it compounds, I can convert one to the other."),
    (["What's the difference between simple and compound interest?", "How is compound interest different from simple interest?", "Explain compound interest."],
     ["simple vs compound interest, what's the difference?"],
     "Simple interest is calculated only on the original amount. Compound interest is calculated on the original amount plus any interest already added, so your balance grows faster over time because you earn interest on your interest. The longer the time period, the bigger the difference."),
    (["What is an index fund?", "How do index funds work?", "Explain index funds to me."],
     ["What exactly is an index fund?"],
     "An index fund is a mutual fund or ETF that tracks a market index, such as the S&P 500, by holding the same securities in the same proportions. Because it isn't trying to pick winners, it usually has low fees and gives you broad diversification in a single investment."),
    (["What's the difference between an ETF and a mutual fund?", "ETF vs mutual fund?", "Are ETFs better than mutual funds?"],
     ["How does an ETF differ from a mutual fund?"],
     "Both pool money from many investors to buy a basket of securities. ETFs trade on an exchange throughout the day like a stock, while mutual funds are bought and sold once a day at the closing price. ETFs are often more tax-efficient and have no minimum beyond one share; mutual funds can make automatic investing easier. Which is better depends on the specific fund's fees and how you invest."),
    (["What's the difference between a Roth IRA and a traditional IRA?", "Roth or traditional IRA, how do they differ?", "How does a Roth IRA work?"],
     ["Explain Roth vs traditional IRAs."],
     "With a traditional IRA, contributions may be tax-deductible now and withdrawals in retirement are taxed as income. With a Roth IRA, you contribute money you've already paid tax on, and qualified withdrawals in retirement are tax-free. A Roth tends to suit people who expect a higher tax rate later; a traditional IRA suits those who expect a lower one. Contribution limits and income rules change, so check the current IRS limits."),
    (["What is a 401(k)?", "How does a 401k work?", "What's an employer match on a 401(k)?"],
     ["Can you explain what a 401(k) plan is?"],
     "A 401(k) is an employer-sponsored retirement account. You contribute part of each paycheck, usually before taxes, and the money grows tax-deferred until you withdraw it in retirement. Many employers match part of your contributions, which is essentially free money, so it's usually worth contributing at least enough to get the full match."),
    (["What does amortization mean?", "What is an amortization schedule?", "Explain loan amortization."],
     ["How does amortization work on a loan?"],
     "Amortization means paying off a loan with regular, equal payments that cover both interest and principal. Early payments are mostly interest; as the balance shrinks, more of each payment goes toward principal. I can work out a loan's payment, or how much you'd still owe after a certain number of payments."),
    (["What is a credit score?", "How do credit scores work?", "What affects my credit score?"],
     ["What goes into a credit score?"],
     "A credit score is a number that summarizes how reliably you've handled credit. It's based mainly on your payment history, how much of your available credit you're using, the age of your accounts, your mix of credit types and recent applications for new credit. Higher scores usually qualify you for lower interest rates."),
    (["What is a bond?", "How do bonds work?", "Why do bond prices fall when interest rates rise?"],
     ["Explain what a bond is."],
     "A bond is a loan you make to a government or company. The issuer pays you interest, called the coupon, on a set schedule and returns the face value when the bond matures. Bond prices move opposite to interest rates: when new bonds pay more, existing bonds with lower coupons become less valuable."),
    (["What is diversification?", "Why should I diversify my investments?", "What does it mean to diversify a portfolio?"],
     ["Why does diversification matter?"],
     "Diversification means spreading your money across many investments, such as different companies, industries, countries and asset types, so that one bad performer doesn't sink your whole portfolio. It reduces risk without necessarily reducing expected returns, which is why broad index funds are popular."),
    (["What is dollar-cost averaging?", "Is dollar cost averaging a good strategy?", "Explain DCA investing."],
     ["What does dollar-cost averaging mean?"],
     "Dollar-cost averaging means investing a fixed amount on a regular schedule regardless of price. You automatically buy more shares when prices are low and fewer when they're high, which lowers your average cost per share and takes the guesswork out of timing the market. Give me your purchase amount and prices and I can work out your average cost."),
    (["What is an emergency fund?", "Why do I need an emergency fund?", "How much should an emergency fund be?"],
     ["What's the point of an emergency fund?"],
     "An emergency fund is cash set aside for unexpected costs like a job loss, a medical bill or a car repair. A common guideline is three to six months of essential expenses, kept somewhere safe and easy to access, such as a high-yield savings account. Tell me your monthly expenses and I can size one for you."),
    (["What is NPV?", "What does net present value mean?", "How do I interpret NPV?"],
     ["Explain net present value."],
     "Net present value (NPV) is the value today of an investment's future cash flows minus its upfront cost, after discounting those future cash flows at your required rate of return. A positive NPV means the investment is expected to earn more than that rate; a negative NPV means it falls short."),
    (["What is IRR?", "What does internal rate of return mean?", "IRR vs NPV, what's the difference?"],
     ["Explain internal rate of return."],
     "The internal rate of return (IRR) is the annual rate at which an investment's net present value equals zero. Think of it as the investment's effective yearly return. If the IRR is higher than your required return or cost of capital, the investment is generally worth considering."),
    (["What is inflation?", "How does inflation affect my savings?", "Why does inflation matter?"],
     ["What does inflation do to money?"],
     "Inflation is the general rise in prices over time, which means each dollar buys a little less every year. Money sitting in cash loses purchasing power whenever inflation is higher than the interest it earns, which is why long-term savings are usually invested."),
    (["What's the difference between stocks and bonds?", "Stocks vs bonds?", "Should I understand stocks before bonds?"],
     ["How are stocks different from bonds?"],
     "A stock is a share of ownership in a company, so its value rises and falls with the company's prospects. A bond is a loan to a company or government that pays fixed interest. Stocks have historically offered higher long-term returns with more ups and downs; bonds are usually steadier but grow more slowly."),
    (["What is a dividend?", "How do dividends work?", "What does it mean when a stock pays dividends?"],
     ["Explain stock dividends."],
     "A dividend is a share of a company's profits paid to its shareholders, usually in cash and often quarterly. Not every company pays one; many growing companies reinvest their profits instead. The dividend yield tells you the annual dividend as a percentage of the share price."),
    (["What is a P/E ratio?", "What does price to earnings mean?", "Is a high P/E ratio bad?"],
     ["How do I read a P/E ratio?"],
     "The price-to-earnings (P/E) ratio is a stock's price divided by its earnings per share. It tells you how much investors are paying for each dollar of a company's annual profit. A high P/E can mean investors expect strong growth or that the stock is expensive; it's most useful when comparing similar companies."),
    (["What is a high-yield savings account?", "Are high yield savings accounts worth it?", "What's an HYSA?"],
     ["How does a high-yield savings account work?"],
     "A high-yield savings account is a savings account, often at an online bank, that pays a much higher interest rate than a typical bank account. Deposits are usually FDIC-insured, and you can withdraw any time, which makes it a good home for an emergency fund or short-term savings."),
    (["What is an expense ratio?", "Why do fund fees matter?", "What does expense ratio mean on a fund?"],
     ["Explain fund expense ratios."],
     "An expense ratio is the annual fee a fund charges, expressed as a percentage of your investment. It's taken out automatically, so you never see a bill, but it reduces your returns every year. Over decades even small differences add up, and I can show you exactly how much a fee costs over time."),
    (["What are capital gains?", "How are capital gains taxed?", "What's the difference between short-term and long-term capital gains?"],
     ["Explain capital gains tax."],
     "A capital gain is the profit you make when you sell an asset for more than you paid. In the U.S., assets held longer than a year usually qualify for lower long-term capital gains rates, while short-term gains are taxed as ordinary income. Tax rules depend on your situation, so check with a tax professional for specifics."),
    (["What is net worth?", "How do I calculate my net worth?", "What does net worth mean?"],
     ["Explain net worth."],
     "Your net worth is everything you own minus everything you owe: your assets, like cash, investments, retirement accounts and home equity, minus your liabilities, like mortgages, loans and credit card balances. Tracking it over time is one of the simplest ways to see your overall financial progress."),
    (["What is the rule of 72?", "How does the rule of 72 work?"],
     ["Explain the rule of 72."],
     "The Rule of 72 is a shortcut for estimating how long it takes money to double: divide 72 by the annual growth rate. At 8%, for example, money doubles in about nine years. Give me a rate and I'll calculate both the estimate and the exact answer."),
    (["What is the Sharpe ratio?", "What does a Sharpe ratio tell me?"],
     ["How do I read a Sharpe ratio?"],
     "The Sharpe ratio measures risk-adjusted return: the portfolio's return above the risk-free rate, divided by its volatility. A higher Sharpe ratio means you're being paid more return for each unit of risk you take, which makes it useful for comparing investments with different risk levels."),
    (["What is a debt-to-income ratio?", "Why do lenders care about DTI?"],
     ["Explain debt-to-income ratio."],
     "Debt-to-income ratio (DTI) is your total monthly debt payments divided by your gross monthly income. Lenders use it to judge whether you can take on more debt; many prefer it at or below 36%, and 43% is a common limit for qualified mortgages. Share your payments and income and I'll calculate yours."),
    (["What is PMI?", "What is private mortgage insurance?", "How do I avoid PMI?"],
     ["Explain PMI on a mortgage."],
     "Private mortgage insurance (PMI) protects the lender if you stop paying a conventional mortgage. It's usually required when your down payment is less than 20% of the home price, adds to your monthly cost, and can typically be removed once you've built enough equity in the home."),
    (["What's the difference between a fixed and an adjustable rate mortgage?", "Fixed vs ARM mortgage?", "How does an adjustable-rate mortgage work?"],
     ["How do fixed-rate and adjustable-rate mortgages differ?"],
     "A fixed-rate mortgage keeps the same interest rate and principal-and-interest payment for the whole loan. An adjustable-rate mortgage (ARM) starts with a fixed rate for a set number of years, then adjusts periodically based on market rates, so your payment can rise or fall. ARMs often start lower but carry more uncertainty."),
    (["What is a CD?", "How does a certificate of deposit work?", "Are CDs a good investment?"],
     ["Explain certificates of deposit."],
     "A certificate of deposit (CD) is a bank account that pays a fixed interest rate in exchange for leaving your money untouched for a set term, from a few months to several years. Withdrawing early usually costs a penalty. CDs are low-risk and usually FDIC-insured, making them good for money you won't need for a while."),
    (["What is an annuity?", "How do annuities work?"],
     ["Explain annuities."],
     "An annuity is a contract, usually with an insurance company, where you pay a lump sum or a series of payments in exchange for regular income, either for a set period or for life. They can provide guaranteed income in retirement but often come with fees and limited access to your money, so read the terms carefully."),
    (["What is depreciation?", "How does straight-line depreciation work?"],
     ["Explain depreciation for a business."],
     "Depreciation spreads the cost of a long-lived asset, like equipment or a vehicle, over the years it's used, instead of expensing it all at once. Straight-line depreciation divides the cost minus salvage value evenly across the asset's useful life. Give me the cost, salvage value and life and I'll calculate it."),
    (["What is a break-even point?", "What does break even mean for a business?"],
     ["Explain break-even analysis."],
     "The break-even point is the sales volume at which revenue exactly covers costs, so you make neither a profit nor a loss. It equals fixed costs divided by the contribution margin per unit, which is the price minus the variable cost of each unit. Every sale beyond that point is profit."),
    (["What's the difference between gross and net income?", "Gross vs net pay?"],
     ["How does gross income differ from net income?"],
     "Gross income is what you earn before anything is taken out. Net income, or take-home pay, is what's left after taxes, retirement contributions, insurance premiums and other deductions. Lenders usually look at gross income, while budgets like the 50/30/20 rule use take-home pay."),
    (["What's the difference between markup and margin?", "Markup vs margin?"],
     ["How are markup and profit margin different?"],
     "Both measure profit, but against different bases. Markup is profit as a percentage of cost, while margin is profit as a percentage of the selling price. The same sale always has a higher markup than margin, so it matters which one someone is quoting."),
    (["What is the 4% rule?", "What is FIRE?", "How does the 4 percent rule work?"],
     ["Explain the 4% rule for retirement."],
     "The 4% rule is a retirement guideline: withdraw about 4% of your savings in the first year, adjust that amount for inflation afterward, and historically your money has lasted around 30 years. It implies saving roughly 25 times your annual spending. FIRE (financial independence, retire early) planners use it to set a savings target. It's a rule of thumb, not a guarantee."),
    (["What is the 50/30/20 rule?", "How does the 50 30 20 budget work?"],
     ["Explain the 50/30/20 budgeting rule."],
     "The 50/30/20 rule is a simple budget: 50% of your take-home pay goes to needs like housing and groceries, 30% to wants like dining out and travel, and 20% to savings and extra debt payments. Tell me your take-home pay and I'll split it for you."),
    (["What is credit utilization?", "How much of my credit limit should I use?"],
     ["Explain credit utilization."],
     "Credit utilization is the share of your available credit card limits that you're currently using. Lower is better for your credit score; keeping it under about 30% is a common guideline, and the lower the better. Paying balances down before the statement closes can help."),
    (["What is refinancing?", "When does it make sense to refinance a mortgage?"],
     ["Explain refinancing."],
     "Refinancing means replacing your current loan with a new one, usually to get a lower interest rate, change the term or switch from an adjustable to a fixed rate. It makes sense when the monthly savings recover the closing costs well before you plan to sell or pay off the loan. I can calculate your break-even point if you share the details."),
    (["What is market volatility?", "What does volatility mean in investing?"],
     ["Explain volatility."],
     "Volatility measures how much an investment's price swings up and down, usually as the standard deviation of its returns. Higher volatility means bigger, less predictable moves in both directions. It's a common way to measure risk, and it's the denominator in the Sharpe ratio."),
    (["What is opportunity cost?", "Explain opportunity cost in personal finance."],
     ["What does opportunity cost mean?"],
     "Opportunity cost is what you give up by choosing one option over another. If you spend money on a new car, the opportunity cost includes what that money could have earned if it had been invested instead. Comparing options this way helps make trade-offs explicit."),
    (["What is a bull market?", "What's the difference between a bull and bear market?"],
     ["Explain bull and bear markets."],
     "A bull market is a period of rising prices and optimism; a bear market is a sustained decline, commonly defined as a drop of 20% or more from a recent high. Both are normal parts of market cycles, and long-term investors usually experience several of each."),
]

